  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule reads: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". Reply A is: ```ruby\n{\"p\": 1}\n```\n{\"q\": 2}\nReply B is: {\"q\": 2}\nFor Reply A the rule requires the answer {\"p\": 1}, because a code fence holds an object and that fenced object is the last one inside any fence. For Reply B the rule requires the answer {\"q\": 2}, because no fence holds an object, so the last top-level object is scored. The check, however, gives the same answer for both: its fence pattern matches only unlabeled, ```json, or ```JSON fences, not ```ruby, so for Reply A the fenced pool is empty and the top-level fallback scans the whole text, finds both {\"p\": 1} and {\"q\": 2}, and returns the last, {\"q\": 2}. Reply B also returns {\"q\": 2}. Thus the check answers unchanged on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the check returned {\"p\": 1} for Reply A and {\"q\": 2} for Reply B, or if the rule were read as counting only json-tagged fences rather than any fence.",
